// XFAIL: DF-153b a private std TYPE reserves its simple name for every program —
// the half of DF-140h that only covered statics
//
// DF-140h gave a module-private `static` inside a std file a module-local
// identity, so a user program may declare its own `ASCII_ZERO` and std still
// reads its own. A module-private TYPE never got that identity: std.file
// declares a private `enum OpenMode` (design 155), so a program declaring its
// own `OpenMode` silently loses to std's — the user's cases are not there, and
// the error names a declaration the author cannot see, import or find:
//
//     error: enum `OpenMode` has no variant `Write`
//
// A struct or enum name is the shape a user is most likely to pick, and std's
// private types are invisible, so the reserved set is unknowable. Design 82
// makes each std FILE its own module and design 144 makes type identity
// `(defining module, name)`; this is that rule not reaching std types.
//
// The same failure blocks the reverse direction inside std: two std files
// cannot declare one name either (DF-153a), which is why design 153's sweep
// could not give std.net's SysError tags the name rt/ABI.md gives them.
//
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT: 1

enum OpenMode: UInt8 {
    case Read = 0,
    case Write = 1
}

func main() {
    print("{OpenMode.Write as UInt8}")
}
